es/bitcoin"},"teaserSnippet":"(Kitco News) - Friday, January 9--January bitcoin futures prices are weaker in early trading Friday. The bulls faded this week. A fledgling price uptrend on the daily bar chart has was negated.","title":"Bitcoin January 9 daily chart alert - Bears have the near-term technical advantage","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/article/2026-01-09/bitcoin-january-9-daily-chart-alert-bears-have-near-term-technical","source":{"name":"Kitco News","subtitle":"The Leading News Source in Precious Metals","description":"\u003cp\u003eKitco NEWS has a diverse team of journalists reporting on the economy, stock markets, commodities, cryptocurrencies, mining and metals with accuracy and objectivity. Our goal is to help people make informed market decisions through in-depth reporting, daily market roundups, interviews with prominent industry figures, comprehensive coverage (often exclusive) of important industry events and analyses of market-affecting developments.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"},"audioTts":null,"createdAt":"2026-01-09T07:14:45-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-09T07:18:23-0500","image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/bae0c25d-908f-4b35-b1ae-3c6ed2616290.webp?top=0.16453382084095\u0026left=0\u0026width=1280\u0026height=719.67093235832\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=f483d338c4e3776c4b3d56f5b4431e26d8cc89527f27423cd5a03fbeb7e7a655"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/bae0c25d-908f-4b35-b1ae-3c6ed2616290.webp?top=0.16453382084095\u0026left=0\u0026width=1280\u0026height=719.67093235832\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=edaaee0178e8d7bca990af41f4c1a0a9d8e56060c62aedc884c521e19a60883a","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/bae0c25d-908f-4b35-b1ae-3c6ed2616290.webp?top=0.16453382084095\u0026left=0\u0026width=1280\u0026height=719.67093235832\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=b7b01c6f70b65163f70300f4b10f50177d68f60dc692941a1b247753d5df5c52","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/bae0c25d-908f-4b35-b1ae-3c6ed2616290.webp?top=0.16453382084095\u0026left=0\u0026width=1280\u0026height=719.67093235832\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=f483d338c4e3776c4b3d56f5b4431e26d8cc89527f27423cd5a03fbeb7e7a655"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/bae0c25d-908f-4b35-b1ae-3c6ed2616290.webp?top=0\u0026left=279.8353909465\u0026width=720.329218107\u0026height=720\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=d9dada29c3600415069194df7c7b0d7cd8b2ab3991840e7afe62ce4bda151ac1"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/bae0c25d-908f-4b35-b1ae-3c6ed2616290.webp?top=0.16453382084095\u0026left=0\u0026width=1280\u0026height=719.67093235832\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=1cb5c149f5a2f22a98e1225310c184779eb9d1305195da7e118a006a9afa8c4c"}}}},"teaserImage":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":null},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":null,"media":null},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":null,"media":null},"desktop":{"media":null,"srcset":null},"mobile":{"media":null,"srcset":null},"tablet":{"media":null,"srcset":null}}}},"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null}]},"cacheTagsHeader":null},"dataUpdateCount":1,"dataUpdatedAt":1769807182960,"error":null,"errorUpdateCount":0,"errorUpdatedAt":0,"fetchFailureCount":0,"fetchFailureReason":null,"fetchMeta":null,"isInvalidated":false,"status":"success","fetchStatus":"idle"},"queryKey":["newsByCategoryGeneric",{"limit":15,"offset":0,"urlAlias":"/news/category/cryptocurrencies"}],"queryHash":"[\"newsByCategoryGeneric\",{\"limit\":15,\"offset\":0,\"urlAlias\":\"/news/category/cryptocurrencies\"}]"},{"dehydratedAt":1769807184065,"state":{"data":{"nodeListByCategory":{"total":2419,"items":[{"id":216960,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eKazakh gold miner Solidcore Resources said on Friday it expects its gold output to rise 37% to 540,000 ounces of gold equivalent in 2026, after a fall last year.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe company’s 2025 gold equivalent production was down by 19% year-on-year to 395,000 oz., below its guidance of 420,000 oz.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eSolidcore said its gold equivalent sales in 2025 amounted to 412,000 oz., down 23% year-on-year. But revenue increased by 13% to $1.5 billion, driven by higher gold prices which compensated for the lower sales.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Anastasia Lyrchikova; Editing by Mark Trevelyan)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"Solidcore sees 37% growth in gold output in 2026","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"Kazakh gold miner Solidcore Resources said on Friday it expects its gold output to rise 37% to 540,000 ounces of gold equivalent in 2026, after a fall last year.The company’s 2025 gold equivalent production was down by 19% year-on-year to 395,000 oz., below its guidance of 420,000 oz.Solidcore said its gold equivalent sales in 2025 amounted to 412,000 oz., down 23% year-on-year. But revenue increased by 13% to $1.5 billion, driven by higher gold prices which compensated for the lower sales.(By Anastasia Lyrchikova; Editing by Mark Trevelyan)","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-30/solidcore-sees-37-growth-gold-output-2026","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-30T15:26:14-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-30T15:27:29-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/7122a25c-b0c1-47a9-8394-236a288ff086.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=d577fed176b349492a008b7e6b6fb9a604a40cc7f32dd97a7a6bda194a1b0bdf"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/7122a25c-b0c1-47a9-8394-236a288ff086.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=ee8d838917bbf94b3b71cf6745cc7edfda4b7370397f5b2b9485a327b376e0da","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/7122a25c-b0c1-47a9-8394-236a288ff086.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=add7ed183727e530ff922a3424bc0491555734975cb650e82bdb40caa3351096","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/7122a25c-b0c1-47a9-8394-236a288ff086.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=d577fed176b349492a008b7e6b6fb9a604a40cc7f32dd97a7a6bda194a1b0bdf"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/7122a25c-b0c1-47a9-8394-236a288ff086.jpeg?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=09c50d73f3befd0dc9b330ada7a94acaeb8a33d7f8310942feffdf5effb51442"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/7122a25c-b0c1-47a9-8394-236a288ff086.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=a69b0931a091e25cbdf9a7b0a0b16708bf798286d0ee93d032988ad7786fe044"}}}},"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":216956,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eImpala Platinum said on Friday its refined platinum group metal (PGM) output was stable at 1.78 million ounces during the first half of its financial year, but revenue rose 40%, helped by firmer metal prices.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003ePrices of major PGMs have rebounded since the second half of 2025, driving Impala’s sales revenue to 33,250 rand ($2,092.08) per ounce during the six months to December 2025, from 23,831 rand an ounce in the prior period.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eImpala said in a production update it remains on track to meet its annual production target of 3.4-3.6 million ounces.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe Johannesburg-based miner will publish its half-year financial results on March 5.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e($1 = 15.8933 rand)\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Nelson Banya; Editing by Rashmi Aich)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"Impala Platinum posts flat half-year refined output","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"Impala Platinum said on Friday its refined platinum group metal (PGM) output was stable at 1.78 million ounces during the first half of its financial year, but revenue rose 40%, helped by firmer metal prices.Prices of major PGMs have rebounded since the second half of 2025, driving Impala’s sales revenue to 33,250 rand ($2,092.08) per ounce during the six months to December 2025, from 23,831 rand an ounce in the prior period.Impala said in a production update it remains on track to meet its annual production target of 3.4-3.6 million ounces.","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-30/impala-platinum-posts-flat-half-year-refined-output","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-30T15:01:05-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-30T15:02:00-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b36f076b-1936-4917-9aa4-49c32366f349.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=b0d495ebe8a8e2d96f38ffae1b974e3f9f73781883614bd2ddbb642d2c7e37c7"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b36f076b-1936-4917-9aa4-49c32366f349.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=7d8380c4c33432d5b6f6b70c975ef7e3d5e81fcc75d0e76772c3b192fa1c0c45","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b36f076b-1936-4917-9aa4-49c32366f349.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=fd64a16393c2bcce348b0d025a28a65fa8e3528c99e699fd14018d25953fe31c","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b36f076b-1936-4917-9aa4-49c32366f349.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=b0d495ebe8a8e2d96f38ffae1b974e3f9f73781883614bd2ddbb642d2c7e37c7"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b36f076b-1936-4917-9aa4-49c32366f349.jpeg?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=f73e103cab0863152357766ece9cbe48ce1cde87ceff0e45ca92657e7ae67c2c"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b36f076b-1936-4917-9aa4-49c32366f349.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=ea68dcd68cd00071b9bd262a6e33fc5288990ad8b17172e33e276e6c89dea6ee"}}}},"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":216954,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eAustralia on Friday said it would support its critical mineral supply chains after the US stepped back from plans to guarantee a minimum price for such projects.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eShares of Australia’s rare earth miners fell sharply on Thursday after Reuters reported on the Trump administration’s retreat.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe sector was still in the red on Friday. Lynas, the world’s biggest producer of rare earths outside China, was down by more than 4%.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe backdown was communicated to US mining executives by Trump administration officials and indicated a lack of congressional funding for price floors and the complexity of setting market pricing, Reuters reported.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThat “won’t stop Australia (from) pursuing our critical minerals strategic reserve program to make sure Australia has access to the resources it needs to build a future made in Australia,” Resources Minister Madeleine King told Sky News on Friday.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e“We know from what we’ve seen in reports and we will let that play out … the US has introduced a price floor for one particular project and that’s the only one it has done it for, and that was a game-changer.”\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAustralia has been positioning itself as a critical minerals alternative to China, the world’s biggest producer, for use in the automotive and defence sectors.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIt has said it would establish a A$1.2 billion ($840 million) strategic reserve of minerals that it believes is vulnerable to supply disruption.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe stockpile, which will prioritize antimony, gallium and rare earth elements, is expected to be ready by the second half of 2026.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe government is also considering setting a price floor to support local critical minerals projects as part of its strategy.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e“We’ll have a number of mechanisms, a floor price will be one through offtake agreements,” King said. “We are determined to make sure there is value for taxpayer money in the reserve and in any floor price.”\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e($1 = 1.4278 Australian dollars)\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Christine Chen; Editing by Thomas Derpinghaus)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"Australia says US price floor backdown won’t derail its critical minerals strategy","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"Australia on Friday said it would support its critical mineral supply chains after the US stepped back from plans to guarantee a minimum price for such projects.Shares of Australia’s rare earth miners fell sharply on Thursday after Reuters reported on the Trump administration’s retreat.The sector was still in the red on Friday. Lynas, the world’s biggest producer of rare earths outside China, was down by more than 4%.","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-30/australia-says-us-price-floor-backdown-wont-derail-its-critical","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-30T14:46:20-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-30T14:53:09-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/ae3258af-5460-441a-8d9f-4fd803688077.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=aecf84e240283e0e9d8ed3cc7dc90ab0702612a57404e732c15420d89d6934b9"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/ae3258af-5460-441a-8d9f-4fd803688077.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=a44fa11c056986da30e4fe1b931dcd272674670f53d051346aa8a25476afcf68","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/ae3258af-5460-441a-8d9f-4fd803688077.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=f5e7cb470c6961c705c344a3ce51b7a73b4e2dc8bc3122c5531e8611ea0b888d","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/ae3258af-5460-441a-8d9f-4fd803688077.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=aecf84e240283e0e9d8ed3cc7dc90ab0702612a57404e732c15420d89d6934b9"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/ae3258af-5460-441a-8d9f-4fd803688077.jpeg?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=6352ca08b26693b6cddaaf85afe1ce2a1cd3232caf45ada043a6f0131e35cb43"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/ae3258af-5460-441a-8d9f-4fd803688077.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=34736132076d3311d178ecf8fd7dfef71d48fc4d799a0b80b9d8bed2c3c19bdf"}}}},"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":216953,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eChina’s Baowu Resources, the world’s largest steelmaker, has tightened its grip on one of the biggest untapped high‑grade iron-ore deposits by taking control of the operator of Guinea’s Simandou Blocks 1 and 2 after raising its stake in the Winning Consortium Simandou to 51% from 49%, the company said on Friday.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe Singapore-registered parent company and its Guinean unit have been renamed Baowu Winning Consortium Simandou following the deal, the statement said. It was approved by Guinea on May 30, 2024 and formally completed on January 30, 2026.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eBWCS owns 85% of the Guinean operating company for Blocks 1 and 2, strengthening Beijing’s leverage over Simandou in a country that also exports bauxite and other minerals.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eOn the southern Blocks 3 and 4, Chinese state groups also hold stakes via a Chinalco-led joint venture alongside Rio Tinto and the Guinean state in the Simfer partnership.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eSimfer co-developed shared rail and port infrastructure with BWCS for Simandou, which debuted iron ore shipments in November after nearly three decades of stop-start development, regulatory disputes and infrastructure delays.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eBaowu said the deal confirms its long-term industrial and strategic commitment to “one of the world’s most significant integrated mining and infrastructure projects,” adding it will pursue “project competitiveness, the promotion of local content, (and) compliance with internationally recognized ESG standards.”\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAt full-run rate, Simandou’s two mining hubs are designed to ship up to 120 million metric tons of high-grade iron ore a year through the shared rail and Atlantic port, positioning Guinea as a key supplier of the steelmaking material alongside Australia and Brazil.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eGuinea’s mines ministry did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eGuinea is also the world’s largest bauxite exporter, with Chinese firms controlling over 70% of output.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Maxwell Akalaare Adombila; Editing by Louise Heavens and Chris Reese)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"China’s Baowu takes control of Simandou iron ore operator","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"China’s Baowu Resources, the world’s largest steelmaker, has tightened its grip on one of the biggest untapped high‑grade iron-ore deposits by taking control of the operator of Guinea’s Simandou Blocks 1 and 2 after raising its stake in the Winning Consortium Simandou to 51% from 49%, the company said on Friday.The Singapore-registered parent company and its Guinean unit have been renamed Baowu Winning Consortium Simandou following the deal, the statement said. It was approved by Guinea on May 30, 2024 and formally completed on January 30, 2026.","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-30/chinas-baowu-takes-control-simandou-iron-ore-operator","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-30T14:30:37-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-30T14:38:26-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/c43730f7-4450-4346-a3aa-c77d17173940.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=0e2fc8daeec4475f93ad3c82f23b59c8e83d45040399a60ba135150ac484c40f"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/c43730f7-4450-4346-a3aa-c77d17173940.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=feceb5ebc96b578f3895a7e8811f0ca4bf76b544e5aef30e031ec89a0a23a08b","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/c43730f7-4450-4346-a3aa-c77d17173940.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=739e4fcf80ab20cfa3e992ee65238370cf574af6d9514ede6766a683e1a6d443","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/c43730f7-4450-4346-a3aa-c77d17173940.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=0e2fc8daeec4475f93ad3c82f23b59c8e83d45040399a60ba135150ac484c40f"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/c43730f7-4450-4346-a3aa-c77d17173940.jpeg?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=38cc7cddb1c75d058211e5e4085cf04debbbbbb6270b2c3f8721bd0a7a864259"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/c43730f7-4450-4346-a3aa-c77d17173940.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=0e1ec18d5d18104efc1e873c34d57896d1c83f1da090283abd38ebef59a93ae8"}}}},"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":216875,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eTungsten prices have rocketed to record highs in January, fuelled by tightening inventory, Chinese export controls and industrial demand, leaving consumers scrambling for supplies in an already constricted market.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003ePrices of ammonium paratungstate, used to make tungsten metal, were trading at $1,125 to $1,150 per metric ton unit (mtu) in China, a record high, according to traders. Prices in Rotterdam hit an all-time high of around $1,100. Prices are widely expected to climb further in the coming weeks.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eTungsten is a critical industrial metal, prized for its hardness with the highest melting point of any metal. It is used mainly as tungsten carbide in cutting tools and wear parts in machinery for manufacturing, mining and construction, as well as in aerospace and defence components, industrial gas turbines and electronics.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003ePrice spikes can ripple through factory output and costs, making tungsten a bellwether for advanced manufacturing.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e“There are a lot of reasons for Tungsten to go up – market is tight, there is a lot of demand from defence, aerospace, IGT, and all other sectors, there are problems with supply (lower ore grades and other issues) and on top of that, there are issues with exports from China,” a London-based trader said in an email to Reuters.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eChina, which dominates global tungsten mining and processing, unveiled export controls in February 2025, requiring exporters to get government permits before shipping their materials.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eLast month, Beijing named 15 companies that will be able to export tungsten, a move that could centralize control and limit the volume of material available to overseas buyers.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e“(China’s) export volume has declined by close to 40% year-on-year since the controls were implemented,” said William Parry-Jones, founder of Wolfram Advisory and a specialist in tungsten market analysis and critical materials supply chain strategy. “There are insufficient volumes of raw materials available ex-China to replace this export volume.”\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAccording to 2024 data from the US Geological Survey, tungsten mine production outside China is fragmented, led by Vietnam, Russia and modest production from Rwanda, Bolivia, Austria, and Spain, collectively accounting for only a few thousand tons a year, compared with China’s 67,000 tons.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eMarket participants attributed the higher China prices to the country’s restricted mining quota, which was cut 6.5% from a year earlier in 2025, and a higher rate of manufacturing, leading to more domestic consumption.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e“China has been proactively building its manufacturing capacities – it is now seen as the factory of the world … if as an end-use customer you can’t physically buy your widget from your preferred supplier, then you may have no choice but to buy the widget from China, this plays perfectly into the Chinese plan,” Parry-Jones said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cbr\u003eGlobal tightness intensified more after new Chinese controls on dual-use materials to Japan came into force this month. Japan is one of the largest importers of Chinese tungsten.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Anjana Anil; Editing by Thomas Derpinghaus)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"Tungsten price rises to record highs as export curbs turn up supply heat","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"Tungsten prices have rocketed to record highs in January, fuelled by tightening inventory, Chinese export controls and industrial demand, leaving consumers scrambling for supplies in an already constricted market.Prices of ammonium paratungstate, used to make tungsten metal, were trading at $1,125 to $1,150 per metric ton unit (mtu) in China, a record high, according to traders. Prices in Rotterdam hit an all-time high of around $1,100. Prices are widely expected to climb further in the coming weeks.","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-29/tungsten-price-rises-record-highs-export-curbs-turn-supply-heat","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-29T16:29:36-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-29T16:30:32-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/73558187-0462-4139-ac1c-47ed5df208ac.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=e85241f34f806984709cb7ccde879d2e55a0e129b44c17fb6cff4123f7ce298c"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/73558187-0462-4139-ac1c-47ed5df208ac.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=6a2240b77929752c49f6edfd45c41211e42aaff51e24fd31d083b7648127c3f4","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/73558187-0462-4139-ac1c-47ed5df208ac.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=d52b1860741819cbc8f821b88bb2b44506375e504183feef72b48da8ed94148c","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/73558187-0462-4139-ac1c-47ed5df208ac.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=e85241f34f806984709cb7ccde879d2e55a0e129b44c17fb6cff4123f7ce298c"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/73558187-0462-4139-ac1c-47ed5df208ac.png?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=af12a578b4f0ccb2da60824c2e07d1942adabdaaac75f6360b922157f45d0f68"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/73558187-0462-4139-ac1c-47ed5df208ac.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=280a5b1fd8d71c4473a7916eb30e2701398817291717d94338fcce228aa07bf7"}}}},"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":216874,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eIndia plans to soon offer incentives for companies to set up lithium and nickel processing plants to help boost output and meet rising demand for critical minerals, according to two sources and a government presentation reviewed by Reuters.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIndia is seeking to accelerate its energy transition and cut emissions by promoting clean energy initiatives like electric vehicles, though it lacks the technology to process critical minerals, a capacity that is largely dominated by China.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eNickel and lithium are critical to India’s EV supply chain, especially when it comes to batteries, as New Delhi targets 30% electric car penetration and 80% for two-wheelers by 2030 from 4% and 6% at present.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe incentive plan proposes a 15% capital subsidy for eligible investments in lithium and nickel-processing projects starting on or after April 1, 2026, which would be subject to a cap, according to the presentation.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eA 15% capital subsidy appears to be “realistic,” one of the sources said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIndia’s Ministry of Mines, which is responsible for the proposal, did not respond to a Reuters email seeking comments.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eUnder the program, incentives would be available for five years and capped at 40% of annual net sales turnover for lithium processing plants and 25% for nickel plants, the presentation showed.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eTo qualify for the incentives, lithium processing plants must have a minimum capacity of 30,000 metric tons, while nickel plants must have at least 50,000 tons, it showed.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe subsidy would be disbursed in stages, subject to minimum plant utilization targets set by the government, the presentation showed.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe government initially plans to roll out the incentives for two lithium and two nickel projects to meet the country’s demand by 2030, one of the sources and the presentation said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIn 2023, India identified more than 20 minerals – including lithium – as “critical” for its energy transition efforts and to meet rising demand from businesses.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eReuters previously reported that India approached several countries to seek technical collaborations on lithium processing.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Neha Arora; Editing by Mayank Bhardwaj and Thomas Derpinghaus)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"India to unveil incentives for lithium, nickel processing","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"India plans to soon offer incentives for companies to set up lithium and nickel processing plants to help boost output and meet rising demand for critical minerals, according to two sources and a government presentation reviewed by Reuters.India is seeking to accelerate its energy transition and cut emissions by promoting clean energy initiatives like electric vehicles, though it lacks the technology to process critical minerals, a capacity that is largely dominated by China.","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-29/india-unveil-incentives-lithium-nickel-processing","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-29T16:28:26-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-29T16:29:29-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/527ffb9e-0264-4185-9c7e-9f6ac9e7eabe.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=afbb9e5ebb3446fce41939d00f3035cd589711cfc89aa6f4fa47fd2de7709896"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/527ffb9e-0264-4185-9c7e-9f6ac9e7eabe.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=6d512210f9c3d3d6e0e2aefdc74d55aa6b341d7d7a7734479b1b7d325380fb75","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/527ffb9e-0264-4185-9c7e-9f6ac9e7eabe.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=02929b14b0c33ec9c683cc990de1f5dcb602397403317c51455aef6410ca0996","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/527ffb9e-0264-4185-9c7e-9f6ac9e7eabe.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=afbb9e5ebb3446fce41939d00f3035cd589711cfc89aa6f4fa47fd2de7709896"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/527ffb9e-0264-4185-9c7e-9f6ac9e7eabe.png?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=107a71572cca2e431ab575dba175fa3411747b0f3492f0caaa646b1530d49261"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/527ffb9e-0264-4185-9c7e-9f6ac9e7eabe.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=6df090a9ffe4070da6edd2ca43b3490fc767baacd9b0dc7afa8bdcf3c8bd3f46"}}}},"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":216873,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eSwiss-based Solway Investment Group plans to restart its nickel operations in Guatemala within months, the company told Reuters, following a sharp rise in the nickel price and after the United States removed sanctions on the facilities.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe amounts involved are a small fraction of global nickel supplies, estimated by analysts at around four million metric tons this year.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eBut industry sources say the plan suggests that prices are high enough for some of the smelters that cut output after prices dropped to three-year lows towards the end of 2024, to resume profitable production.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003ePrices of nickel, also used to make electric vehicle batteries, on Monday jumped to a 19-month peak at $19,160 a metric ton on the London Metal Exchange. Nickel has gained nearly 20% since leading producer Indonesia said late last year it planned to cut nickel mining quotas.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eSolway’s operation in Guatemala comprises the PRONICO facility with annual production capacity of 25,000 metric tons of nickel in ferronickel and CGN, a mine with capacity to extract up to 2.2 million tons of nickel ore.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e“The anticipated timeline for restarting CGN is around April to May 2026, aligned with the restart of PRONICO,” Solway said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eSolway closed PRONICO and CGN after the US placed sanctions on its Guatemalan subsidiaries in November 2022. The US lifted them in January 2024.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eFerronickel is trading at around a $2,000 a ton discount to the LME nickel price, according to one industry source, adding that because Solway owns its own mine in Guatemala, its costs of producing the ferronickel would be around $12,000 a ton.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Pratima Desai; Editing by Barbara Lewis)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"Solway to restart Guatemala nickel plant after price surge","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"Swiss-based Solway Investment Group plans to restart its nickel operations in Guatemala within months, the company told Reuters, following a sharp rise in the nickel price and after the United States removed sanctions on the facilities.The amounts involved are a small fraction of global nickel supplies, estimated by analysts at around four million metric tons this year.","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-29/solway-restart-guatemala-nickel-plant-after-price-surge","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-29T16:25:56-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-29T16:27:01-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/25d15250-0b22-4dfd-9062-4d368cc1ee1c.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=45edabd955db363a2187af48aeb911efa984845c9cfb8428aeb5c1b823c98799"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/25d15250-0b22-4dfd-9062-4d368cc1ee1c.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=2ed093e0352806da812834f2b13da0633b63c1200e56d34a9c8d451df8e590e8","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/25d15250-0b22-4dfd-9062-4d368cc1ee1c.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=7c59a0216ce42a4d1c00349a7e07f782c3e67e76870d77465e8ba30c62024e99","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/25d15250-0b22-4dfd-9062-4d368cc1ee1c.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=45edabd955db363a2187af48aeb911efa984845c9cfb8428aeb5c1b823c98799"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/25d15250-0b22-4dfd-9062-4d368cc1ee1c.png?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=22c5cd2b5ebe1fa32803cf81254a7c0f0aca014dea257516f1a4c853cc6b3007"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/25d15250-0b22-4dfd-9062-4d368cc1ee1c.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=25b1ba80db9d35ca8975405f3801ecbdb5708537a5cc539e9e538ecf82bd5833"}}}},"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":216872,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eBrazilian inspection teams found that Vale’s Viga mine presented signs of erosion and ordered the iron-ore miner to shut its operation until further notice, Minas Gerais state government authorities said on Thursday.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eOn Monday, Vale announced it had halted operations at Viga and nearby mine Fabrica after heavy rains caused water overflows that impacted a local river and the operations of neighboring steelmaker CSN.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe suspension at Viga will last until the firm presents reports that show it can re-establish environmental control of the project, said Gustavo Endrigo, superintendent of inspection at Minas Gerais government.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIt is unclear when Vale will be able to resume production at the mines, whose operating permits had also been suspended by the city of Congonhas. The two sites account for about 2% of Vale’s iron ore production outlook for this year, analysts say.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eVale did not immediately reply to a request for comment. It had previously said it was fully cooperating with authorities.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eVale has already received three fines totaling 1.7 million reais ($324,000) by the state government due to Sunday’s overflows, the authorities said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAuthorities said that as well as water, tailings from Vale’s iron ore mining operation at Fabrica had also reached the Maranhao River.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eMore heavy rain is expected in Brazil next week, according to LSEG data, with Minas Gerais expected to be among the hardest hit areas.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eVale has faced intense scrutiny since two major dam disasters in Minas Gerais in the 2010s.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e($1 = 5.2438 reais)\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Fabio Teixeira; Editing by Alison Williams)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"Brazilian authorities find erosion at Vale unit after water overflow","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"Brazilian inspection teams found that Vale’s Viga mine presented signs of erosion and ordered the iron-ore miner to shut its operation until further notice, Minas Gerais state government authorities said on Thursday.On Monday, Vale announced it had halted operations at Viga and nearby mine Fabrica after heavy rains caused water overflows that impacted a local river and the operations of neighboring steelmaker CSN.","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-29/brazilian-authorities-find-erosion-vale-unit-after-water-overflow","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-29T16:08:37-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-29T16:24:36-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/46a67d8f-7db4-4f21-b773-b7f602a88b80.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=ff1a184d58529c154f59d9e08bdd26c936aa3b7bfa4d3e351ba8ad07790c9c3d"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/46a67d8f-7db4-4f21-b773-b7f602a88b80.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=517217d16a9fb1cccae4bbb2da22178af6e16fda0e1531f8781de0781c817a93","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/46a67d8f-7db4-4f21-b773-b7f602a88b80.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=18278d2f2bfbf82d54931b55a4315ede42096e31b0ea6eab1082def168ae6c1c","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/46a67d8f-7db4-4f21-b773-b7f602a88b80.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=ff1a184d58529c154f59d9e08bdd26c936aa3b7bfa4d3e351ba8ad07790c9c3d"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/46a67d8f-7db4-4f21-b773-b7f602a88b80.png?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=ac2fa358da337d149c3f6d0fe0d5818ac5fbf7faccf8522cc55f739fd988e4cb"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/46a67d8f-7db4-4f21-b773-b7f602a88b80.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=834e951173527f941c23456643c6180ac9c33f3f1bd65803d9b0617c956a617c"}}}},"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":216870,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eThe union representing workers at Capstone Copper’s Mantoverde copper and gold mine in northern Chile on Thursday urged workers to reject a contract offer from the firm and prolong a strike that began in early January.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe mine on Wednesday presented a new offer to workers, which the union said was worse than a previous proposal. Employees will vote on the deal on Friday.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Fabian Cambero; Editing by Sarah Morland)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"Union for Chile’s Mantoverde mine calls on workers to reject contract offer","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"The union representing workers at Capstone Copper’s Mantoverde copper and gold mine in northern Chile on Thursday urged workers to reject a contract offer from the firm and prolong a strike that began in early January.The mine on Wednesday presented a new offer to workers, which the union said was worse than a previous proposal. Employees will vote on the deal on Friday.(By Fabian Cambero; Editing by Sarah Morland)","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-29/union-chiles-mantoverde-mine-calls-workers-reject-contract-offer","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-29T16:07:05-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-29T16:08:25-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/5dcf4e3d-ba9c-4bc3-b860-c66e616814d4.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=7aed7ba5e38703957724678fb5e79413b22c249377b5dc6fd28f7cd1c0273282"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/5dcf4e3d-ba9c-4bc3-b860-c66e616814d4.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=4e7a45391158545c727133b5a2e737f16cf707c9667ad6e69678c1dcbe629528","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/5dcf4e3d-ba9c-4bc3-b860-c66e616814d4.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=9901cc1ab5fa765d41bb5a95bd0416e2026420dc4add95a09b6dca513cc5f47f","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/5dcf4e3d-ba9c-4bc3-b860-c66e616814d4.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=7aed7ba5e38703957724678fb5e79413b22c249377b5dc6fd28f7cd1c0273282"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/5dcf4e3d-ba9c-4bc3-b860-c66e616814d4.png?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=8fbf883016a15f9b68d27602d5e95aff0ac47e97b876be16b3b6a55792a837f1"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/5dcf4e3d-ba9c-4bc3-b860-c66e616814d4.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=a0d0383ace35e8456d900c0427ce4dd81c10189b7eaf6203c6ad9f35457ddeed"}}}},"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":216869,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eGold demand hit an all-time high last year as jitters over instability and trade sparked a surge in investment, even as a series of record price peaks kept jewellery buyers from the tills.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eGlobal gold demand rose by 1% in 2025 to 5,002 metric tons, the highest number on record, the World Gold Council said on Thursday.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003ePrices of the metal jumped above $5,300 per ounce for the first time on Wednesday and are up 22% so far this year after a 64% jump in 2025 on safe-haven demand driven by geopolitical tensions and more recently weakening confidence in the US dollar.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e“The biggest question this year will be whether investment demand is going to be strong enough to maintain the strength of the gold market,” said John Reade, senior market strategist at the World Gold Council.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eGold investment at record\u003cbr\u003eThe WGC expects another year of strong inflows into gold-backed exchange-traded funds and robust demand for bars and coins. ETFs saw inflows of 801 tons of gold in 2025, while demand for bars and coins jumped 16% to a 12-year high.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eOverall gold investment demand soared 84% to a record high of 2,175 tons in 2025.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eHowever, the WGC expects record-high prices to hit jewellery demand this year and will slow down purchases by central banks to 850 tons from 863 tons in 2025, even though their buying remains elevated when compared to the pre-2022 level.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eGold jewellery demand fell 18% in 2025, with buying in China down 24% at its lowest since 2009.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAfter a gold price rally and active purchases by central banks in 2022-2025, the share of gold in foreign currency holdings around the globe is now approaching early-1990s levels, “a period with more concentrated ownership and arguably fewer incentives for gold ownership than today,” the WGC said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIts estimate is based on officially reported gold purchases by central banks and an assessment of unreported buying by consultancy Metals Focus.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e“Many banks manage gold outside their formal reserve frameworks; some likely target volumes rather than values. This makes it ineffective to identify a single ‘satiation point’ for aggregate demand,” the WGC added.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Polina Devitt; Editing by Jan Harvey)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"Global gold demand hits record high in 2025, WGC says","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"Gold demand hit an all-time high last year as jitters over instability and trade sparked a surge in investment, even as a series of record price peaks kept jewellery buyers from the tills.Global gold demand rose by 1% in 2025 to 5,002 metric tons, the highest number on record, the World Gold Council said on Thursday.Prices of the metal jumped above $5,300 per ounce for the first time on Wednesday and are up 22% so far this year after a 64% jump in 2025 on safe-haven demand driven by geopolitical tensions and more recently weakening confidence in the US dollar.","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-29/global-gold-demand-hits-record-high-2025-wgc-says","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-29T16:02:37-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-29T16:06:55-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/fefebe17-5b81-4755-bd27-6a1149704df9.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=da815719e536d18cdea2a607487861c729618dc7bdd15aaadf5a5ee70788188e"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/fefebe17-5b81-4755-bd27-6a1149704df9.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=623e151a55d2b9901fa9cd0dd7420dae1263315b86f5678e0d8c7d81c85ebb89","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/fefebe17-5b81-4755-bd27-6a1149704df9.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=9f3500b561754d3180f7014c5de2544f65d66aa9fc407e49de9fb2d65f399d78","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/fefebe17-5b81-4755-bd27-6a1149704df9.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=da815719e536d18cdea2a607487861c729618dc7bdd15aaadf5a5ee70788188e"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/fefebe17-5b81-4755-bd27-6a1149704df9.png?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=026ec1582cea9f2f71437a7d887fdcead433cb6556f86d0bd99e37beebd73418"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/fefebe17-5b81-4755-bd27-6a1149704df9.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=2b5c64bf1bce8125135454f5ff4df218ec681ff404a1297221e6968f236779c5"}}}},"featured":true,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":216759,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eGrupo Mexico, one of the world’s largest copper producers, nearly doubled its fourth-quarter net profit on higher metals prices and stronger volumes, it said on Tuesday, more than offsetting weaker infrastructure results tied to suspended oil platforms and foreign-exchange headwinds.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe conglomerate, which last year became the largest listed company on the Mexican market, saw net profits reach $1.43 billion, above the $1.32 billion estimate from analysts polled by LSEG.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eEarnings were boosted by increased production, with copper output climbing 2% and gold jumping 14%. Prices also leaped, with copper up 22%, silver up 74% and gold up 56%, Grupo Mexico said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eRevenues for the group climbed 34% to $5.15 billion, above analysts’ $4.87 billion estimate.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe miner is eyeing billions of dollar in investments over the next decade, with projects pending at home and in the US, Peru and Spain.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eGrupo Mexico said its US unit, Asarco, could double mine production due to new copper policies outlined by President Donald Trump. It added that it was moving forward with an assessment to reopen and expand its Hayden smelter and Amarillo refinery in the US.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIn Mexico, the firm said it is in talks with the current administration to resume more than $10 billion in investments after permits were paused under the previous government.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eGrupo Mexico’s infrastructure division swung to a net loss in the quarter, weighing down results, pressured by the suspension of four of offshore oil platforms by state-owned producer Pemex and foreign-exchange effects.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Kylie Madry; Editing by Natalia Siniawski)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"Grupo Mexico’s net profit doubles in fourth quarter on metals boom","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"Grupo Mexico, one of the world’s largest copper producers, nearly doubled its fourth-quarter net profit on higher metals prices and stronger volumes, it said on Tuesday, more than offsetting weaker infrastructure results tied to suspended oil platforms and foreign-exchange headwinds.The conglomerate, which last year became the largest listed company on the Mexican market, saw net profits reach $1.43 billion, above the $1.32 billion estimate from analysts polled by LSEG.","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-28/grupo-mexicos-net-profit-doubles-fourth-quarter-metals-boom","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-28T13:37:42-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-28T13:38:49-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/4eed6850-c4b5-40fd-b808-ba2571160d17.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=283eb7acd974c5a2c479dc8be2901f18b16aa44cfebb1de167a5960e057cbe41"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/4eed6850-c4b5-40fd-b808-ba2571160d17.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=2956f811cf04859989bfcf9fc0f67b4db69276bda301771c60a1fd3bc46a3d00","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/4eed6850-c4b5-40fd-b808-ba2571160d17.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=ba024f55708b9644a7b1dceeb46cce4f28f81ca3760a571d8898bff2dd1899a6","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/4eed6850-c4b5-40fd-b808-ba2571160d17.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=283eb7acd974c5a2c479dc8be2901f18b16aa44cfebb1de167a5960e057cbe41"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/4eed6850-c4b5-40fd-b808-ba2571160d17.png?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=b6a12fa736bfc0f49cf0c42837155a74cc56fafb1051859186b39124510e5db6"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/4eed6850-c4b5-40fd-b808-ba2571160d17.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=29589fab794e5a2a8d6d35a26f44935e7674c72c2067d104db08e778f646c84e"}}}},"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":216758,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eRussia’s Nornickel, the world’s largest palladium producer and a major producer of refined nickel, said on Wednesday it expects its nickel output to reach 193,000–203,000 metric tons in 2026, broadly in line with 2025.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIts palladium output this year is expected to fall to between 2.415 million and 2.465 million ounces, the company added, after it produced 2.725 million ounces of palladium in 2025, down 1% year-on-year but in line with its forecasts.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe company said it produced 198,521 tons of nickel in 2025, a 3% drop from the previous year, a result that also came within the company’s projected range of 196,000 to 204,000 tons.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eNornickel has linked a decline in production in recent years to the depletion of ore reserves and a forced switch to alternative mining equipment due to Western sanctions, and expects recovery in 2028.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eWhile the company is not subject to direct sanctions, the measures have restricted access to Western equipment, complicated payments and logistics, and prompted some foreign clients to avoid buying Russian metal, leading it to redirect sales to Asia.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e“The company slightly decreased the output of all key metals, except for platinum, as a result of a temporary increase in the share of disseminated ores, with low-grade ores sparse,” chief operating officer Evgeniy Fedorov said in a statement.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eNornickel has continued upgrading its mining equipment fleet with new suppliers after losing access to Western equipment, he added.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe company has launched construction of a deeper level at its Skalisty mine to bring rich ores from the Oktyabrskoye deposit into production, it said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e“It is this project that will allow us to restore the share of rich ore in total output by 2028,” Fedorov said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAs a result, output could increase by around 20% in nickel equivalent by 2030, the company’s CEO Vladimir Potanin said earlier, calling the current decline in production a “temporary phenomenon”.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe company sees steady demand for its products and does not plan to artificially cut output, Fedorov added.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Anastasia Lyrchikova; Editing by Louise Heavens and Jan Harvey)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"Nornickel sees 2026 nickel output broadly in line with previous year","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"Russia’s Nornickel, the world’s largest palladium producer and a major producer of refined nickel, said on Wednesday it expects its nickel output to reach 193,000–203,000 metric tons in 2026, broadly in line with 2025.Its palladium output this year is expected to fall to between 2.415 million and 2.465 million ounces, the company added, after it produced 2.725 million ounces of palladium in 2025, down 1% year-on-year but in line with its forecasts.","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-28/nornickel-sees-2026-nickel-output-broadly-line-previous-year","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-28T13:35:35-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-28T13:36:52-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/101369b1-d07e-420b-bbee-5f059659888d.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=fa97d8885360bb0da0074ea4c0919d534bfeea31a2df34dbbb942e6ff3388eb0"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/101369b1-d07e-420b-bbee-5f059659888d.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=ec2c7abfdb731a9435bdcefd2be000c9515cf6949fe144e95e81fdb6e41b8756","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/101369b1-d07e-420b-bbee-5f059659888d.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=f743f9066bb489da6d37ea74a0701178d8ebb69fcee7fdd37bc34e70adaec222","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/101369b1-d07e-420b-bbee-5f059659888d.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=fa97d8885360bb0da0074ea4c0919d534bfeea31a2df34dbbb942e6ff3388eb0"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/101369b1-d07e-420b-bbee-5f059659888d.png?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=40aeb442971b81283bd8e90754e8fc9a5a9ef0a1e6473a219c47b2c7450ea27b"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/101369b1-d07e-420b-bbee-5f059659888d.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=ae37bcd8fda602dd31841ce829bff0c8d4fa5501e816fa9f7bd8887518224c8a"}}}},"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":216757,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eFrench rare earths specialist Carester and Malaysian miner Malaco Mining Group have teamed up to work on a rare earth separation plant, and will also collaborate on rare earth mining, an adviser to French President Emmanuel Macron said in Kuala Lumpur on Wednesday.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe cooperation agreement on the separation plant project, which is in its “pilot stage”, will include technology transfers to Malaco as well as expertise to ensure environmental compliance, said Benjamin Gallezot, an adviser on strategic minerals, after meeting with Malaysian government officials.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eWestern countries are trying to develop sources of rare earths, used in electric vehicles, smartphones and other high-tech products, to reduce reliance on China, the world’s largest rare earth producer.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eMalaysia is looking to foreign partners to help develop its domestic rare earth industry. The country has an estimated 16.1 million tonnes of rare earth deposits but lacks the technological capacity to mine and process them.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eGallezot said the companies would seek to build a Malaysian plant focused on rare earth separation, a process needed before the elements can be used to make advanced magnets for the manufacture of electric vehicles and clean technologies.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eMalaco was already in touch with several European magnet makers, Gallezot said, declining to identify the companies. The Japanese government has also expressed interest in the joint project, he said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eMalaco Mining did not respond to a request for comment made outside office hours. The Japanese embassy in Malaysia also did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eSeparately, Gallezot said G7 countries would look to hold discussions with foreign partners outside the group on critical minerals during its meetings this year.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e“We consider that to diversify the supply chain, international cooperation is key,” he said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Rozanna Latiff; Editing by David Stanway and Jane Merriman)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"France’s Carester teams up with Malaysian miner on rare earth project","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"French rare earths specialist Carester and Malaysian miner Malaco Mining Group have teamed up to work on a rare earth separation plant, and will also collaborate on rare earth mining, an adviser to French President Emmanuel Macron said in Kuala Lumpur on Wednesday.The cooperation agreement on the separation plant project, which is in its “pilot stage”, will include technology transfers to Malaco as well as expertise to ensure environmental compliance, said Benjamin Gallezot, an adviser on strategic minerals, after meeting with Malaysian government officials.","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-28/frances-carester-teams-malaysian-miner-rare-earth-project","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-28T13:18:48-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-28T13:35:14-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/9bd93288-eafa-40bd-a91c-15360fb67409.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=8bb38b39dd50b666c34e12d011ee0bb383481e72161566c6d9ee1732d989776f"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/9bd93288-eafa-40bd-a91c-15360fb67409.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=c5dd94c0dfbec56ca68fe155460c0d25bb38933c94587387f5a291889d50735b","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/9bd93288-eafa-40bd-a91c-15360fb67409.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=8ee5c09e19b4ce1ff87d38a47011d08bf7eb7055cc4e1e4cc6be2a5ca898d726","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/9bd93288-eafa-40bd-a91c-15360fb67409.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=8bb38b39dd50b666c34e12d011ee0bb383481e72161566c6d9ee1732d989776f"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/9bd93288-eafa-40bd-a91c-15360fb67409.png?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=4feb79886bc91152bee5bd06698045d5c0617fd59a681211ec84ca1529173ee8"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/9bd93288-eafa-40bd-a91c-15360fb67409.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=80f70ea23c4f61234c19d22a0edaaa24d9d5be874140285bf2ae49569e7f1e4a"}}}},"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":216756,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eIndia is considering tightening mining auction rules to weed out developers that delay seeking clearances to mine on blocks they win at auctions, two sources familiar with the matter told Reuters, as the country seeks to speed up minerals production.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eSince 2015, the government has auctioned 594 mineral blocks, out of which less than 14% or 82 blocks were operational, as per official data.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAs part of an internal assessment, India has identified around 50 companies that have not begun mining and in some cases have not yet sought approvals to do so on their awarded blocks over the last five years and may blacklist them from participating in future auctions, the sources said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eBoth sources declined to be identified as they were not authorized to speak to the media. The federal Ministry of Mines did not respond to an email seeking comments.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIndia plans to assess financials of a mining firm alongside past experience before allowing it to bid, one of the sources said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e“We will make the selection criteria more stringent so only renowned companies can participate. This is still in discussion stage,” the source said, adding that it could take a few months before any changes were finalized.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIn October, the mines ministry publicly notified penalties to be levied on miners that delayed seeking clearances to operationalize blocks won at auctions.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eLast year, the government said it was concerned about slow development of greenfield iron ore mines.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIndia lags behind major economies such as Australia in mining’s contribution to the total share of gross domestic product, which hovers close to only 2%.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e“This is due to a combination of factors, such as regulatory and environmental challenges in obtaining approvals and clearances, inadequate and inefficient infrastructure and investment and technological constraints, among others,” said a joint government and industry report released last year.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Neha Arora; Editing by Jamie Freed)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"India considers tighter mining auction rules to deter laggards","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"India is considering tightening mining auction rules to weed out developers that delay seeking clearances to mine on blocks they win at auctions, two sources familiar with the matter told Reuters, as the country seeks to speed up minerals production.Since 2015, the government has auctioned 594 mineral blocks, out of which less than 14% or 82 blocks were operational, as per official data.","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-28/india-considers-tighter-mining-auction-rules-deter-laggards","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-28T13:08:47-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-28T13:18:29-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b016bf8a-8998-437e-bbcb-3504457394a3.png?top=80\u0026left=0\u0026width=1536\u0026height=864\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=15fb5f602127766ccaa339a1bbbcff8237e3b33faf80f6c5c31a5a876e1a2c4c"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b016bf8a-8998-437e-bbcb-3504457394a3.png?top=80\u0026left=0\u0026width=1536\u0026height=864\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=bfe1e8db2bf51864d568b40f399296c90adb871085143a7f617dd47a988d53e2","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b016bf8a-8998-437e-bbcb-3504457394a3.png?top=80\u0026left=0\u0026width=1536\u0026height=864\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=94a8d9db7290327200a41af9093e959bb35250571d11e4e8b9c1e1c08281bc4c","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b016bf8a-8998-437e-bbcb-3504457394a3.png?top=80\u0026left=0\u0026width=1536\u0026height=864\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=15fb5f602127766ccaa339a1bbbcff8237e3b33faf80f6c5c31a5a876e1a2c4c"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b016bf8a-8998-437e-bbcb-3504457394a3.png?top=0\u0026left=256\u0026width=1024\u0026height=1024\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=e200586aeb7db81816ab4fc65a23731b1f151fea9b11b7ec96ac87b3b1046ebf"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b016bf8a-8998-437e-bbcb-3504457394a3.png?top=80\u0026left=0\u0026width=1536\u0026height=864\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=510ebb3764c345d221bc836fb495a4e3b6f4f302c0b823afef701d7fd0a44acd"}}}},"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":216648,"__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eMali has detained five managers from the local entity operating the Yanfolila gold mine over alleged breaches of the country’s mining code, two sources told Reuters.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe employees from Societe des Mines de Komana (SMK) were detained on Friday for allegedly failing to repatriate foreign currency from export revenues, which is required under the mining code, said the sources.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eSMK could not be reached for comment by telephone or email.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe code, which raises taxes and seeks to hand over higher stakes in mining assets to the state, has sparked disputes with operators since it was brought in in 2023, including a two-year standoff with Barrick Mining before a deal was struck in November.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe government said in December it had recovered 761 billion CFA francs ($1.2 billion) in revenue shortfalls from miners following its strict implementation.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAll five of the detained are Malian, said the sources, one of whom is close to the company, while the other is familiar with the matter. Both requested anonymity because they were not authorized to speak to the media.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eA spokesperson for Mali’s justice ministry did not immediately respond to a request for comment. The mines ministry spokesperson said the ministry did not have information on the issue.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eWest Africa-focused lender Coris Bank International – operating through a subsidiary, Nioko Resources – acquired the Yanfolila mine from UK-based Hummingbird Resources in 2025.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe mine produced 83,965 ounces of gold in 2023, with 493,000 ounces in reserves, according to its profile.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eMali increased its stake in SMK to 20% in 2025 in line with its revised mining code.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(By Tiemoko Diallo, Maxwell Akalaare Adombila and Portia Crowe; Editing by Robbie Corey-Boulet and Jan Harvey)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"title":"Mali detains five gold mine managers over alleged code breaches","teaserHeadline":null,"teaserSnippet":"Mali has detained five managers from the local entity operating the Yanfolila gold mine over alleged breaches of the country’s mining code, two sources told Reuters.The employees from Societe des Mines de Komana (SMK) were detained on Friday for allegedly failing to repatriate foreign currency from export revenues, which is required under the mining code, said the sources.SMK could not be reached for comment by telephone or email.","urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-27/mali-detains-five-gold-mine-managers-over-alleged-code-breaches","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-01-27T15:10:22-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-27T15:13:02-0500","imageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/5bb0a23d-4e7a-4ca6-a590-6b8c683c726f.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=ffcc5d9ef1da9f2e0a46fa307c1b2d9ac9dcf34fdfc0c149510e1c3126c8fdf2"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/5bb0a23d-4e7a-4ca6-a590-6b8c683c726f.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=f0f9b3660dff51a707ddaffd40924b07865cca2f928ae18721225051c4486a0c","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/5bb0a23d-4e7a-4ca6-a590-6b8c683c726f.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=87458602fcdf3ec648a5374dd13464b8590e5db08aec61cdec9ec35c82039808","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/5bb0a23d-4e7a-4ca6-a590-6b8c683c726f.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=ffcc5d9ef1da9f2e0a46fa307c1b2d9ac9dcf34fdfc0c149510e1c3126c8fdf2"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/5bb0a23d-4e7a-4ca6-a590-6b8c683c726f.png?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=ef187d8d14cfbd2e24227e1d51b39803edece8863708dba1a244cf48af891de4"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/5bb0a23d-4e7a-4ca6-a590-6b8c683c726f.png?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=f9516704162ac3d250aa972c5a67df4588a698589fe3404446c2f15fd30abc47"}}}},"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null}]},"cacheTagsHeader":null},"dataUpdateCount":1,"dataUpdatedAt":1769807182985,"error":null,"errorUpdateCount":0,"errorUpdatedAt":0,"fetchFailureCount":0,"fetchFailureReason":null,"fetchMeta":null,"isInvalidated":false,"status":"success","fetchStatus":"idle"},"queryKey":["newsByCategoryGeneric",{"limit":15,"offset":0,"urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"}],"queryHash":"[\"newsByCategoryGeneric\",{\"limit\":15,\"offset\":0,\"urlAlias\":\"/news/category/mining\"}]"},{"dehydratedAt":1769807184065,"state":{"data":{"nodeList":{"total":40491,"items":[{"id":216960,"title":"Solidcore sees 37% growth in gold output in 2026","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-30/solidcore-sees-37-growth-gold-output-2026","createdAt":"2026-01-30T15:26:14-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-30T15:27:29-0500","author":{"email":"legacy-642@kitco.com","name":"Reuters","imageUrl":null,"urlAlias":"/author/reuters"},"source":{"id":112,"description":null,"name":"Reuters"},"teaserSnippet":"Kazakh gold miner Solidcore Resources said on Friday it expects its gold output to rise 37% to 540,000 ounces of gold equivalent in 2026, after a fall last year.The company’s 2025 gold equivalent production was down by 19% year-on-year to 395,000 oz., below its guidance of 420,000 oz.Solidcore said its gold equivalent sales in 2025 amounted to 412,000 oz., down 23% year-on-year. But revenue increased by 13% to $1.5 billion, driven by higher gold prices which compensated for the lower sales.(By Anastasia Lyrchikova; Editing by Mark Trevelyan)","legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/7122a25c-b0c1-47a9-8394-236a288ff086.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=d577fed176b349492a008b7e6b6fb9a604a40cc7f32dd97a7a6bda194a1b0bdf"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/7122a25c-b0c1-47a9-8394-236a288ff086.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=ee8d838917bbf94b3b71cf6745cc7edfda4b7370397f5b2b9485a327b376e0da","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/7122a25c-b0c1-47a9-8394-236a288ff086.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=add7ed183727e530ff922a3424bc0491555734975cb650e82bdb40caa3351096","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/7122a25c-b0c1-47a9-8394-236a288ff086.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=d577fed176b349492a008b7e6b6fb9a604a40cc7f32dd97a7a6bda194a1b0bdf"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/7122a25c-b0c1-47a9-8394-236a288ff086.jpeg?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=09c50d73f3befd0dc9b330ada7a94acaeb8a33d7f8310942feffdf5effb51442"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/7122a25c-b0c1-47a9-8394-236a288ff086.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=a69b0931a091e25cbdf9a7b0a0b16708bf798286d0ee93d032988ad7786fe044"}}}},"featuredContent":null,"tags":[],"audioTts":null,"summaryBullets":[]},{"id":216956,"title":"Impala Platinum posts flat half-year refined output","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-30/impala-platinum-posts-flat-half-year-refined-output","createdAt":"2026-01-30T15:01:05-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-30T15:02:00-0500","author":{"email":"legacy-642@kitco.com","name":"Reuters","imageUrl":null,"urlAlias":"/author/reuters"},"source":{"id":112,"description":null,"name":"Reuters"},"teaserSnippet":"Impala Platinum said on Friday its refined platinum group metal (PGM) output was stable at 1.78 million ounces during the first half of its financial year, but revenue rose 40%, helped by firmer metal prices.Prices of major PGMs have rebounded since the second half of 2025, driving Impala’s sales revenue to 33,250 rand ($2,092.08) per ounce during the six months to December 2025, from 23,831 rand an ounce in the prior period.Impala said in a production update it remains on track to meet its annual production target of 3.4-3.6 million ounces.","legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b36f076b-1936-4917-9aa4-49c32366f349.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=b0d495ebe8a8e2d96f38ffae1b974e3f9f73781883614bd2ddbb642d2c7e37c7"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b36f076b-1936-4917-9aa4-49c32366f349.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=7d8380c4c33432d5b6f6b70c975ef7e3d5e81fcc75d0e76772c3b192fa1c0c45","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b36f076b-1936-4917-9aa4-49c32366f349.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=fd64a16393c2bcce348b0d025a28a65fa8e3528c99e699fd14018d25953fe31c","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b36f076b-1936-4917-9aa4-49c32366f349.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=b0d495ebe8a8e2d96f38ffae1b974e3f9f73781883614bd2ddbb642d2c7e37c7"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b36f076b-1936-4917-9aa4-49c32366f349.jpeg?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=f73e103cab0863152357766ece9cbe48ce1cde87ceff0e45ca92657e7ae67c2c"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b36f076b-1936-4917-9aa4-49c32366f349.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=ea68dcd68cd00071b9bd262a6e33fc5288990ad8b17172e33e276e6c89dea6ee"}}}},"featuredContent":null,"tags":[],"audioTts":null,"summaryBullets":[]},{"id":216954,"title":"Australia says US price floor backdown won’t derail its critical minerals strategy","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-30/australia-says-us-price-floor-backdown-wont-derail-its-critical","createdAt":"2026-01-30T14:46:20-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-30T14:53:09-0500","author":{"email":"legacy-642@kitco.com","name":"Reuters","imageUrl":null,"urlAlias":"/author/reuters"},"source":{"id":112,"description":null,"name":"Reuters"},"teaserSnippet":"Australia on Friday said it would support its critical mineral supply chains after the US stepped back from plans to guarantee a minimum price for such projects.Shares of Australia’s rare earth miners fell sharply on Thursday after Reuters reported on the Trump administration’s retreat.The sector was still in the red on Friday. Lynas, the world’s biggest producer of rare earths outside China, was down by more than 4%.","legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/ae3258af-5460-441a-8d9f-4fd803688077.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=aecf84e240283e0e9d8ed3cc7dc90ab0702612a57404e732c15420d89d6934b9"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/ae3258af-5460-441a-8d9f-4fd803688077.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=a44fa11c056986da30e4fe1b931dcd272674670f53d051346aa8a25476afcf68","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/ae3258af-5460-441a-8d9f-4fd803688077.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=f5e7cb470c6961c705c344a3ce51b7a73b4e2dc8bc3122c5531e8611ea0b888d","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/ae3258af-5460-441a-8d9f-4fd803688077.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=aecf84e240283e0e9d8ed3cc7dc90ab0702612a57404e732c15420d89d6934b9"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/ae3258af-5460-441a-8d9f-4fd803688077.jpeg?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=6352ca08b26693b6cddaaf85afe1ce2a1cd3232caf45ada043a6f0131e35cb43"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/ae3258af-5460-441a-8d9f-4fd803688077.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=34736132076d3311d178ecf8fd7dfef71d48fc4d799a0b80b9d8bed2c3c19bdf"}}}},"featuredContent":null,"tags":[],"audioTts":null,"summaryBullets":[]},{"id":216953,"title":"China’s Baowu takes control of Simandou iron ore operator","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-30/chinas-baowu-takes-control-simandou-iron-ore-operator","createdAt":"2026-01-30T14:30:37-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-30T14:38:26-0500","author":{"email":"legacy-642@kitco.com","name":"Reuters","imageUrl":null,"urlAlias":"/author/reuters"},"source":{"id":112,"description":null,"name":"Reuters"},"teaserSnippet":"China’s Baowu Resources, the world’s largest steelmaker, has tightened its grip on one of the biggest untapped high‑grade iron-ore deposits by taking control of the operator of Guinea’s Simandou Blocks 1 and 2 after raising its stake in the Winning Consortium Simandou to 51% from 49%, the company said on Friday.The Singapore-registered parent company and its Guinean unit have been renamed Baowu Winning Consortium Simandou following the deal, the statement said. It was approved by Guinea on May 30, 2024 and formally completed on January 30, 2026.","legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/c43730f7-4450-4346-a3aa-c77d17173940.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=0e2fc8daeec4475f93ad3c82f23b59c8e83d45040399a60ba135150ac484c40f"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/c43730f7-4450-4346-a3aa-c77d17173940.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=feceb5ebc96b578f3895a7e8811f0ca4bf76b544e5aef30e031ec89a0a23a08b","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/c43730f7-4450-4346-a3aa-c77d17173940.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=739e4fcf80ab20cfa3e992ee65238370cf574af6d9514ede6766a683e1a6d443","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/c43730f7-4450-4346-a3aa-c77d17173940.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=0e2fc8daeec4475f93ad3c82f23b59c8e83d45040399a60ba135150ac484c40f"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/c43730f7-4450-4346-a3aa-c77d17173940.jpeg?top=0\u0026left=85.925925925926\u0026width=292.14814814815\u0026height=292\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=38cc7cddb1c75d058211e5e4085cf04debbbbbb6270b2c3f8721bd0a7a864259"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/c43730f7-4450-4346-a3aa-c77d17173940.jpeg?top=15.566176470588\u0026left=0\u0026width=464\u0026height=260.86764705882\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=0e1ec18d5d18104efc1e873c34d57896d1c83f1da090283abd38ebef59a93ae8"}}}},"featuredContent":null,"tags":[],"audioTts":null,"summaryBullets":[]},{"id":216952,"title":"Canada budget deficit over first eight months of 2025/26 rises to C$26.39 bln","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-01-30/canada-budget-deficit-over-first-eight-months-202526-rises-c2639-bln","createdAt":"2026-01-30T14:24:21-0500","updatedAt":"2026-01-30T14:25:22-0500","author":{"email":"legacy-642@kitco.com","name":"Reuters","imageUrl":null,"urlAlias":"/author/reuters"},"source":{"id":112,"description":null,"name":"Reuters"},"teaserSnippet":"OTTAWA, Jan 30 (Reuters) - Canada recorded a slightly higher C$26.39 billion ($19.51 billion) budget ​deficit for the first eight ‌months of the 2025/26 fiscal year as government expenditures grew faster than revenues, the finance ministry said on ‌Friday.","legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/a38de551-3f2a-4d0d-b3a0-9fa10f80323c.webp?top=0.16453382084095\u0026left=0\u0026width=1280\u0026height=719.67093235832\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=a4cc24beca2b1b7322f6949364ac48c429e52ceb8d7cd28cc734cabcc3043381"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/a38de551-3f